kubectl apply is a declarative Kubernetes command used to create or update or delete cluster resources based on a configuration file (usually YAML or JSON). Instead of telling Kubernetes how to change an object step-by-step, you define the desired end state in a file, and kubectl apply figures out what needs to be created, modified, or removed to match that state.

How kubectl apply Works Under the Hood

To make intelligent updates without wiping out changes made by other controllers (like Horizontal Pod Autoscalers), kubectl apply performs a three-way merge using three versions of the resource:

• Local Configuration File: The YAML or JSON file you are currently passing to the command.

• Last-Applied Configuration: A JSON string stored inside the live object's metadata (kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation) from the previous time apply was run.

• Live Object Configuration: The current state of the resource running in the Kubernetes cluster, including fields added dynamically by Kubernetes itself.

By comparing these three versions, Kubernetes calculates a patch:

1. Added fields: Present in your local file, missing from the live object → Added to the live object.
2. Modified fields: Changed in your local file compared to the last-applied annotation → Updated in the live object.
3. Deleted fields: Present in the last-applied annotation, removed from your local file → Deleted from the live object.
4. Untouched dynamic fields: Present in the live object, never existed in the last-applied annotation → Left alone safely.

