What kubectl apply Actually Stores

• Client-Side Apply: It saves a serialized JSON copy of your entire file inside the resource's kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation. It does not attach a version tag or timestamp to this text.

• Server-Side Apply (--server-side): Instead of saving the whole file, the Kubernetes API server tracks which manager (such as kubectl) owns which specific fields inside the object's metadata.managedFields section.



Apply a single file:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Apply all manifests in a directory, if multiple configuration files are there under a directory, they will apply at once
kubectl apply -f /path/to/config-files/

Apply recursively across subdirectories
kubectl apply -R -f ./infrastructure/

Preview changes before applying (Dry Run)
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=server

Check differences between local file and live cluster
kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml

Prune orphaned resources: Deletes resources in the cluster that are no longer present in your local directory (use with caution).

kubectl apply -f ./manifests/ --prune -l app=my-app


Important Best Practices

• Do not mix imperative and declarative commands: Avoid running kubectl edit or kubectl replace on resources managed by kubectl apply, as this causes the last-applied-configuration annotation to drift out of sync.
• Server-Side Apply (--server-side): Modern Kubernetes versions support Server-Side Apply (kubectl apply --server-side -f file.yaml). Instead of relying on a bulky client-side annotation, the Kubernetes API server itself tracks field ownership directly in the resource's managedFields metadata, preventing conflicts when multiple controllers edit the same object.
